#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "851fb5f7-f80f-53fa-a8aa-a4858b37bea6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288797} {:answer/id #uuid "a5abc364-4dcd-50d2-acf2-dfcce2d06a37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288798} {:answer/id #uuid "1c1e2821-38b6-54ad-a344-903cbb502127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288799} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd513d4-0551-51cc-9d9a-ae6737d7be35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288800} {:answer/id #uuid "b0dea2bc-0164-5177-ab7c-1de240772aff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0dea2bc-0164-5177-ab7c-1de240772aff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288801} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f54ef8c-f6d9-5953-9471-6bb05a0111cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43085691-e6df-59c5-9601-1a937f0551cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-96988" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $2x-{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$\n- $3{y}^{′}-2{e}^{x}y={y}^{2},\\,y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=1$\n- $2x{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{x}={y}^{′},\\,y(2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I  is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{y}^{′}-2{e}^{x}y={y}^{2}$ together with the initial condition $y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=1.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2x{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{x}={y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
